Theorem 4.1. For a subgroup N≤G the following are equivalent:
gNg−1⊆N for all g∈G;
gNg−1=N for all g∈G;
gN=Ng for all g∈G;
every left coset of N is a right coset of N.
Proof. (1 ⇒ 2) Apply (1) to g−1: g−1Ng⊆N. Conjugating by g gives N⊆gNg−1. (2 ⇒ 3) gN=gNg−1g=Ng. (3 ⇒ 4) Clear. (4 ⇒ 1) Suppose gN=Nx for some x. Since g∈gN, g∈Nx, so Nx and Ng are right cosets sharing g, hence equal. So gN=Ng. Then for n∈N, gn∈Ng, so gn=n′g for some n′∈N and gng−1=n′∈N. ■
Watch out: gN=Ng is an equality of sets. It does not say gn=ng for each n; it says gn=n′g for some possibly different n′∈N. Normal subgroups need not commute elementwise with G.
